.TH LINUXSCP 1 "2026-10-12" "LinuxSCP 1.0" "LinuxSCP"
.SH NAME
linuxscp \- secure file transfer client for the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linuxscp
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ sftp:// [ user [: password ]@] host [: port ]/ path ]
.br
.B linuxscp
.B \-batch
.BI \-script= file
.br
.B linuxscp
.B \-batch
.BI \-command= commands
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B linuxscp
is the console file transfer client of LinuxSCP. It connects to an SFTP
server over SSH, and transfers files to and from the local file system,
either from a script, from a command line, or interactively.
.PP
It is the native POSIX port of the LinuxSCP application: the SSH and SFTP
protocol code is the PuTTY core that the Windows build uses, driven by a
POSIX front end.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-script= file
Read the commands to run from
.IR file ,
one command per line. Use
.B \-
to read from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-command= commands
Run
.IR commands .
Several commands may be given, separated by
.BR ; .
.TP
.BI \-hostkey= fingerprint
Accept only the host key with this fingerprint. Use
.B *
to accept any key. Without this option an unknown host key is accepted only
after the client has been able to ask; in
.B \-batch
mode the connection fails instead.
.TP
.BI \-privatekey= file
Private key to authenticate with. The PuTTY core expects a PuTTY
.B .ppk
file; OpenSSH and PEM keys are rejected. Use an ssh-agent instead for those.
.TP
.BI \-username= name
User name to authenticate with.
.TP
.BI \-password= password
Password to authenticate with. The
.B LINUXSCP_PASSWORD
environment variable is used when this option is not given, which keeps the
password out of the command line.
.TP
.BI \-port= number
Port to connect to. The default is 22.
.TP
.BI \-timeout= seconds
Connection timeout. The default is 15 seconds.
.TP
.BI \-log= file
Write the session log to
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-batch
Never ask a question. Anything that would need an answer fails instead.
.TP
.B \-verbose
Print what the SSH connection layer does as it happens.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-help
Print the usage and exit.
.SH SITES
A site is a URL of the form
.PP
.RS
.BI sftp:// [ user [: password ]@] host [: port ]/ path
.RE
.PP
The schemes
.BR sftp:// ,
.B ssh://
and
.B scp://
are all accepted, as is a bare
.IR host [ :port ].
The path is the directory the client changes to after connecting. The
password is optional in the URL; prefer
.B \-password=
or
.B LINUXSCP_PASSWORD
so that it is not written into a script file.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI open " site"
Connect to
.IR site .
.TP
.B close
Disconnect.
.TP
.BI cd " directory"
Change the remote directory.
.TP
.B pwd
Print the remote directory.
.TP
.BI ls " [" \-al1 "] [" directory ]
List the remote directory.
.B \-l
is the long format, which is the default;
.B \-1
prints names only;
.B \-a
is accepted and does nothing, as entries starting with a dot are always
listed.
.TP
.BI lcd " directory"
Change the local directory.
.TP
.B lpwd
Print the local directory.
.TP
.BI get " remote local"
Download a file, or every file matching a mask.
.TP
.BI put " local remote"
Upload a file, or every file matching a mask.
.TP
.BI rm " file"
Remove remote files.
.TP
.BI rmdir " directory"
Remove a remote directory.
.TP
.BI mkdir " directory"
Create a remote directory.
.TP
.BI mv " from to"
Move or rename a remote file.
.TP
.BI chmod " permissions file"
Change the permissions of a remote file, given in octal.
.TP
.BI stat " file"
Print the attributes of a remote file.
.TP
.BI option " name value"
Change a transfer option.
.TP
.B help
List the commands.
.TP
.B exit
End the session. The aliases
.BR bye ,
.B quit
and
.B end
also work.
.PP
Local and remote paths may contain the wildcards
.B *
and
.BR ? .
.PP
.B call
and
.B show
are rejected: the connection runs the SFTP subsystem only, so there is no
remote shell to run a command in.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
The keys held by a running ssh-agent are offered automatically when
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
is set, which is the simplest way to authenticate with an OpenSSH key.
.TP
.B LINUXSCP_PASSWORD
Password to use when
.B \-password=
is not given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero if every command succeeded, non-zero otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
Upload everything in the current directory and list the result:
.PP
.RS
.nf
linuxscp \-batch \-command="open sftp://user@example.com/var/www \\
  \-hostkey=SHA256:abcdef... ; put * . ; ls ; exit"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cat > sync.txt <<'EOF'
open sftp://user@example.com/var/www \-hostkey=*
get *.html /var/www/html
exit
EOF
linuxscp \-batch \-script=sync.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
The POSIX client speaks SFTP only. The SCP protocol, FTP, FTPS, WebDAV and
Amazon S3 sessions, local-to-local transfers, resuming interrupted
transfers and the graphical interface of the Windows client are not ported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sftp (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh\-agent (1)
.PP
The Debian port is documented in
.I docs/debian.md
of the source package.
